# Semantic Search vs Keyword Search — Comparison Notes

Reference notes for the RAG & MCP Fundamentals project. Based on real
output from `labs/keyword_search.py` running against `data/policies.json`.

---

## The core difference

**Keyword search** (TF-IDF, BM25) matches exact words. It scores a document
higher if the query's words literally appear in it, weighted by how rare
those words are across the whole collection.

**Semantic search** (embeddings) matches *meaning*. It converts text into
vectors (lists of numbers) that capture concepts, so "money back for gym"
can match "wellness allowance" even though they share zero words.

---

## Pros and Cons

### Keyword Search (TF-IDF / BM25)

| Pros | Cons |
|---|---|
| Fast, no GPU or model download needed | Fails on paraphrased queries (zero word overlap = zero score) |
| Exact matches are precise (great for IDs, SKUs, names, error codes) | Can't tell synonyms apart ("reimbursement" vs "money back") |
| Easy to explain/debug — you can see exactly why a doc scored high | Common words across many docs get little useful signal, sometimes noisy on tiny datasets |
| No embedding cost, works fully offline, no API key | Doesn't understand context or intent, just word overlap |
| Great for structured/technical text (logs, code, product catalogs) | Ranking quality drops as vocabulary overlaps between docs increase |

### Semantic Search (Embeddings)

| Pros | Cons |
|---|---|
| Understands meaning, paraphrases and synonyms work | Slower — needs an embedding model to run on every query |
| Great for natural language questions ("how do I get money back for gym") | Less transparent — harder to explain *why* something matched |
| Works even with zero shared vocabulary between query and doc | Can occasionally match on vague "vibes" rather than a real answer |
| Scales well with vector databases (Chroma, Pinecone) for large corpora | Needs a model download (or API calls) and more setup |
| Captures relationships (e.g. "car" ~ "vehicle" ~ "automobile") | Struggles with exact-match needs (IDs, codes, rare exact terms) |

**In production, most systems use both** (hybrid search): keyword search
for exact-match precision, semantic search for recall on natural language,
then merge/re-rank the results.

